Dining Philosophers Problem

This problem was proposed by Edsger W. Dijkstra to explain resource sharing and synchronization issues.

This problem teaches:

✅ Resource Allocation
✅ Synchronization
✅ Starvation
✅ Deadlock

and serves as the gateway to the Deadlock chapter.

The Story

Imagine:

5 Philosophers

sitting around a round table.

Arrangement:

        P1
     /      \
   F1        F2

 P5            P2

 F5            F3

   P4        P3
      \    /
        F4

Where:

P = Philosopher
F = Fork
Rules

Each philosopher alternates between:

Thinking
Eating
Thinking
Eating
...

To eat, a philosopher needs:

Left Fork
+
Right Fork

Both forks are required.

Example:

Philosopher P1 needs:

F1 + F2

Philosopher P2 needs:

F2 + F3

Notice:

Forks are shared resources.
Resource Sharing

Fork F2 is shared by:

P1
P2

Fork F3 is shared by:

P2
P3

and so on.

Normal Situation

Suppose only P1 is hungry.

P1 picks:

F1
F2

Eats.

Returns forks.

Everything works perfectly.

The Problem Starts

Now suppose:

All 5 philosophers become hungry simultaneously.

Each philosopher picks up their left fork.

Result:

P1 has F1
P2 has F2
P3 has F3
P4 has F4
P5 has F5

Table now:

P1 waiting for F2
P2 waiting for F3
P3 waiting for F4
P4 waiting for F5
P5 waiting for F1

Nobody can continue.

Visualization
P1 waits for P2
P2 waits for P3
P3 waits for P4
P4 waits for P5
P5 waits for P1

This forms a circle.

Result

Everyone waits forever.

Nobody eats.

Nobody releases forks.

System is stuck.

This is called:

Deadlock
What is Deadlock?
Definition

A deadlock occurs when a set of processes are permanently waiting for resources held by each other.

Easy Definition

Think:

I am waiting for you.
You are waiting for me.

Nobody moves.

Real-Life Example

Imagine a road intersection.

Car A waits for Car B.

Car B waits for Car C.

Car C waits for Car D.

Car D waits for Car A.

Result:

Traffic Freeze

Deadlock.

Why Is Dining Philosophers Important?

Because forks represent:

Resources

and philosophers represent:

Processes or Threads

OS faces the same problem.

Example:

Process A needs Printer + Scanner

Process B needs Scanner + Printer

Possible deadlock.

Four Possible States
1. Thinking
No forks needed.
2. Hungry
Wants forks.
3. Picking Forks
Trying to acquire resources.
4. Eating
Using resources.
Main Goal

Ensure:

Everyone eventually eats.

without:

Deadlock
Starvation
Solution 1: Limit Philosophers

Suppose only:

4 philosophers

can attempt to eat simultaneously.

Now at least one philosopher can always get both forks.

Deadlock avoided.

Solution 2: Resource Ordering

Number forks:

F1
F2
F3
F4
F5

Rule:

Always pick lower-numbered fork first.

Then:

No circular waiting.

Deadlock avoided.

Example

P5 needs:

F5 and F1

Instead of:

F5 first

P5 takes:

F1 first

because:

1 < 5

This breaks the circular chain.

Solution 3: One Philosopher Does Opposite

Rule:

P1-P4:
Left then Right

P5:
Right then Left

Now circular waiting cannot form.

Deadlock avoided.

Solution 4: Waiter (Monitor)

Introduce a waiter.

Think restaurant.

Before eating:

Ask Waiter

Waiter checks:

Are both forks available?

If yes:

Permission granted.

Otherwise:

Wait.

This is similar to:

Monitor
Condition Variables

that we studied earlier.

Starvation Problem

Deadlock is not the only issue.

Suppose:

P1 always gets forks.

P2 keeps waiting.

P3 keeps waiting.

Forever.

This is:

Starvation
Difference Between Deadlock and Starvation
Deadlock	Starvation
Everyone waits	One/few wait
Nobody progresses	Others may progress
System frozen	System running
Circular dependency	Unfair scheduling

Example:

Deadlock:

Nobody eats.

Starvation:

P1 eats repeatedly.
P2 never eats.
Operating System Analogy

Philosophers:

Processes

Forks:

Resources

Examples:

Printers
Files
Memory
Database locks

Eating:

Resource Usage

Thinking:

Normal Execution
